{
  "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "query": "US Treasury 10 year yield inversion recession fears August 2019 trade war tariffs List 4",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2018-09-18",
      "to": "2019-09-18"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.forbes.com/sites/jjkinahan/2019/08/14/yield-curve-inversion-disappointing-data-pummels-market-sentiment/",
            "title": "Yield Curve Inversion, Disappointing Data Pummels Market Sentiment",
            "snippets": [
              "# Yield Curve Inversion, Disappointing Data Pummels Market Sentiment\n- Spread between the 2-year and 10-year Treasuries inverts\n- Cboe Volatility Index (VIX) climbs above 20\n- Financials may struggle with yield curve inversion\n\nWell, that didn't last long. Less than a day after the market got a shot in the arm on the tariff front, warning signs here and abroad seem to be flashing \"recession.\"",
              "Meanwhile, a potential recession signal for the United States flashed red, as the yield on the 10-year Treasury slipped below that of the 2-year, a so-called inversion. Also, the yield on the 30-year treasury hit an all-time low as investors moved money into the relative safety of longer-term U.S. government debt",
              "Investors have been watching the 2-year-10-year spread because it has preceded most recessions since WWII. However, it isn’t a perfect predictor, and much of the buying in the 10-year Treasury recently may be trade-based rather than solely from worry about the economy. And investors may also want to keep in mind that there tends to be a lag between an inversion and a recession, and during that time the market can actually gain ground",
              "## Stocks Found Relief After Some Tariffs Delayed\nThis morning’s reversal in sentiment comes a day after the stock market got some relief as the U.S. said it would delay the 10% tariff facing some goods from China from Sept. 1 to Dec. 15, meaning items like cell phones and laptops won’t face the duty during most of the holiday shopping season. Other items were removed from the tariff list altogether",
              "## Tech, Consumer Discretionary Sectors Benefit\nThe Consumer Discretionary sector was the second-best performer of the day, led by Best Buy (BBY) and helped by Nike (NKE) and Hasbro (HAS) as the delayed tariff list included video game consoles and computer monitors as well as certain toys, footwear and clothing"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2019/05/31/us-bonds-fresh-trade-tensions-and-recession-fears.html",
            "title": "US 10-year yield collapses to 2.13% as Trump's surprise Mexico ...",
            "snippets": [
              "U.S. government debt yields added to steep May declines after President Trump's tariffs on all Mexican imports took investors by surprise.\nlogo\nBusiness\nTech\nVideo\nPRO",
              "# US 10-year yield collapses to 2.13% as Trump's surprise Mexico tariffs stoke recession worries\nPublished Fri, May 31 2019 2:37 AM EDT Updated Fri, May 31 2019 4:12 PM EDT\n\nthumbnail\n\nThomas Franck@tomwfranck\n\nWATCH LIVE\n\nU.S. government debt yields added to steep May declines on Friday after President Donald Trump's new tariff threats on all Mexican imports took investors by surprise and aggravated an already-stressed U.S. trade outlook",
              "At 4:07 p.m. ET, the yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was lower at around 2.135%, off a fresh 20-month low around 2.125% hit earlier in the session. A portion of the yield curve remained inverted as the yield on the 3-month Treasury bill held at 2.351%",
              "The 10-year German bund yield hit a low of -0.213%, its lowest level ever back through records that began in 1988.\n\nThe flight to safety on Friday came after Trump said the night before that he will impose a 5% tariff on all Mexican imports starting June 10 if Mexico didn't take unspecified steps to shore up the border, potentially undermining the recently negotiated deal between the U.S., Canada and Mexico",
              "trump tweet\n\n\"While the essential ingredient to the overnight rally is unquestionably the President's threat of tariffs on Mexican goods unless illegal immigration is stopped, the broader backdrop is equally as supportive for Treasuries,\" Ian Lyngen, head of U.S. rate strategy at BMO Capital Markets, wrote in an email",
              "The stall in U.S.-China talks triggered — in addition to the more expensive imports — a paradigm shift in Wall Street's upbeat markets and economic outlook. Traders who had been betting on a lukewarm to positive resolution with China punished risk assets like equities in favor of safer assets like U.S. Treasurys, sending the prices of debt higher and yields lower",
              "The S&P 500 — down 6.5% this month — tracked a 34-basis-point drop in the 10-year Treasury rate, which in turn inverted a portion of the yield curve. Such inversions are often seen as a recession indicator as those who buy debt from the U.S. government for years are compensated with poorer rates than those who loan money for a matter of months",
              "The difference between the rate on the 3-month Treasury bill and the 10-year Treasury note was last seen at -21.8 basis points. The spread between the 10-year yield and the 2-year yield remained at a positive, albeit flattish 21 basis points",
              "But while market focus on Friday centered on Trump's latest tariffs, other bond strategists noted what the larger change in outlook could mean for the Federal Reserve. A growing number of investors now expect the Fed to cut its overnight lending rate later in 2019 as the boost from Trump's tax cuts gives way to mounting trade disputes and weaker economic data overall",
              "\"At the beginning of the year, I said that the 10 year was heading to a 2.25% yield, in my opinion, and maybe even through that. I now believe we are heading to a 2.00% yield for the 10 year Treasury,\" Mark Grant, chief global strategist of fixed income at B. Riley FBR said in an email",
              "\"Much discussion is made of this suggesting a Recession is forthcoming but that is not my take,\" Grant added. \"The Fed, for its own portfolio, has not bought Treasury Bills, a policy that I think will be reversed, and then the Yield Curve will begin to normalize as the Fed's assets include the shorter end of the Curve.\""
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://eu.usatoday.com/story/money/2019/08/14/recession-warning-treasury-yields-invert-stir-economy-fears/2006895001",
            "title": "Recession warning: Treasury yields invert, stir economy fears",
            "snippets": [
              "# A possible recession? Treasury yields invert, sounding economic alarm bells\nAssociated Press\n\nNEW YORK – An economic alarm bell has sounded in the U.S., sending warning signs of a possible recession ahead.\n\nYields on two-year and 10-year Treasury notes inverted early Wednesday, a market phenomenon that shows investors want more in return for short-term government bonds than they are for long-term bonds",
              "It's the first time that has happened since the Great Recession and it can be an indication that investors have lost faith in the soundness of the U.S. economy.\n\nWhat appeared to be a slight thaw in trade relations between the U.S. and China that had sent markets sharply higher Tuesday was quickly forgotten Wednesday.\n\n**Stock market:**Global shares mixed after US plan to delay tariffs on China",
              "The yield on the benchmark 10-year Treasury note hit 1.622%, falling below the yield of a 2-year, which was 1.634%. The last inversion of this part of the yield curve was in December 2005, two years before a recession brought on by the financial crisis hit",
              "An inversion like the one taking place Wednesday has preceded the last nine recessions dating back to 1955, though it doesn't always mean recession is imminent.\n\nAnd when a recession might hit, if it does, is tricky. Months or even years have passed after an inversion takes place, and before economists can connect the two.\n\nStill, the inversion sent a ripple through U.S. markets Wednesday",
              "Longer-term yields have been falling due to anxiety that President Donald Trump's trade war could derail the global economy.\n\n**Heatwave:**It's so hot that teachers are pleading for fan donations in Baltimore\n\nOther parts of the curve had already inverted, beginning late last year. But economists in each instance have cautioned not to make too much of it",
              "The latest inversion, however, is considered more of a tripwire.\n\nUncertainty over what comes next in the trade war could unnerve businesses and consumers enough that they begin cutting spending. Consumer spending accounts for about 70% of economic activity in the U.S.\n\nThat could start a vicious cycle where companies cut back on hiring, which feeds through to more damage for the economy."
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2019/08/23/us-bonds-treasury-yields-rise-as-investors-await-clues-from-powell-speech.html",
            "title": "Yield curve inverts again Friday as Trump reignites fears the trade ...",
